00:16:27.873 --> 00:16:30.933
It comes from internal drive, but

00:16:30.933 --> 00:16:36.850
it's also about how you are seeing
the desire to build a business.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:41.960
And good entrepreneurs just want
to build something big, right?

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.815
You're not coming and say,
I just want to solve a problem.

00:16:43.815 --> 00:16:46.003
There are many people that
want to solve problems.

00:16:46.003 --> 00:16:49.238
But these are people who are saying,
I want to solve a problem at scale.

00:16:49.238 --> 00:16:51.628
I want to solve a problem
with a group of people.

00:16:51.628 --> 00:16:52.530
I want to build,

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:57.331
I want to have people around me that are
equally passionate about building things.

00:16:57.331 --> 00:17:00.894
That energy, that passion sort
of is the starting point.

00:17:00.894 --> 00:17:03.258
&gt;&gt; So they're not just a lone wolf.

00:17:03.258 --> 00:17:06.150
And they don't just have a compelling
problem they want to solve.

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:10.802
They have a growth vision, and they at
least have some people who are with them.

00:17:10.802 --> 00:17:12.003
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:17:12.003 --> 00:17:15.366
&gt;&gt; And so you're not looking at
the numbers at all at first, or have you

00:17:15.366 --> 00:17:19.155
already done a bunch of due diligence
before they even get in the front door?

00:17:19.155 --> 00:17:23.447
&gt;&gt; From a seed and series A perspective,
it is less about numbers and

00:17:23.447 --> 00:17:25.172
it's more about people.

00:17:25.172 --> 00:17:30.914
Numbers really start coming into
account in series B and series C.

00:17:30.914 --> 00:17:32.609
But at a seed and series A,

00:17:32.609 --> 00:17:36.617
very few companies will have
numbers that you can depend on for

00:17:36.617 --> 00:17:39.898
making a decision on a long term basis
&gt;&gt; Right, I mean,

00:17:39.898 --> 00:17:43.904
they'll put something in front of you,
but you both know that it's vapor.

00:17:43.904 --> 00:17:47.952
&gt;&gt; What we do look for is size of market.

00:17:47.952 --> 00:17:52.792
So for us it's very important that you're
planning to target a large market.

00:17:52.792 --> 00:17:57.529
And the reason why I say planning versus
saying you're targeting a large market is

00:17:57.529 --> 00:18:00.701
because there is a third part of this,
which is focus.

00:18:00.701 --> 00:18:04.327
So you're passionate about solving a
problem, you know that you're going after

00:18:04.327 --> 00:18:06.963
a large market, but
you're doing it in a very focused way.

00:18:06.963 --> 00:18:11.874
So sometimes what ends up happening is,
the focus can make it appear

00:18:11.874 --> 00:18:16.198
that the market is small because
you've defined an issue.

00:18:16.198 --> 00:18:21.774
You're going after the top 100,000
customers that really need audio books,

00:18:21.774 --> 00:18:22.321
right?

00:18:22.321 --> 00:18:26.676
And it may appear like a small market, but
you can walk me through and say okay, if I

00:18:26.676 --> 00:18:31.694
get these 100,000, that I can get the next
million, and then I can get the 5 million.

00:18:31.694 --> 00:18:37.544
So that's the third thing is focus, and
many entrepreneurs struggle with that.

00:18:37.544 --> 00:18:42.769
That ability to walk me through
why what you're doing right now,

00:18:42.769 --> 00:18:47.533
will allow you the right to get
to the next set of customers.

00:18:47.533 --> 00:18:52.616
How will you be able to take the learnings
from your 3 billion in revenue and

00:18:52.616 --> 00:18:55.367
scale up to 10, 20, 50, right?

00:18:55.367 --> 00:18:57.677
This is my growth horizon
to get to this point, and

00:18:57.677 --> 00:19:01.042
then I'm going to go on to the next
growth horizon and the next one, right?

00:19:01.042 --> 00:19:04.766
And an entrepreneur that's thinking
that way is also very well

00:19:04.766 --> 00:19:09.412
aligned from an investor perspective
because that's how investments work.

00:19:09.412 --> 00:19:13.250
You start by saying, okay, my first thing
is, I need to get to product market fit.

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:17.448
The next thing is,
I need to get to a GTM fit.

00:19:17.448 --> 00:19:21.906
I need to be able to have a go to
market model which is repeatable,

00:19:21.906 --> 00:19:24.040
which is scalable.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:26.902
The next thing is, I'm now going
to be able to protect my moat,

00:19:26.902 --> 00:19:28.847
whatever I've created as a moat, right?

00:19:28.847 --> 00:19:33.413
So at each stage, you are thinking
through what is the nature of

00:19:33.413 --> 00:19:36.616
problem you're solving in your business.

00:19:36.616 --> 00:19:39.680
That a good entrepreneur is
able to sort of outline that.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:44.253
Third is or the fourth thing
is the ability to motivate.

00:19:44.253 --> 00:19:48.628
That individual when they're presenting
to me, should get me as excited about

00:19:48.628 --> 00:19:52.952
the problem as they are because that's
what they are going to do day in, day out.

00:19:52.952 --> 00:19:54.085
Once they have the company and

00:19:54.085 --> 00:19:57.182
they're out in the market, they're going
to be trying to convince customers.

00:19:57.182 --> 00:20:00.528
They're going to try and
convince the best talent and employees.

00:20:00.528 --> 00:20:04.778
They will be trying to convince the best
partners to work with them, right?

00:20:04.778 --> 00:20:06.776
So they have to get me convinced.

00:20:06.776 --> 00:20:08.805
It's not sales, but
it's motivation, right?

00:20:08.805 --> 00:20:14.163
It's getting that trust built in the other
person that, hey, I can make this happen.

00:20:14.163 --> 00:20:18.117
And the final thing is
the ability to listen and adapt.

00:20:18.117 --> 00:20:22.457
What you need to be able to, and
this is what we test in the conversation,

00:20:22.457 --> 00:20:24.391
is will throw them a curveball.

00:20:24.391 --> 00:20:29.308
We will ask a question which would get
you upset or which will sort of say, hey,

00:20:29.308 --> 00:20:31.332
this just can't work, right?

00:20:31.332 --> 00:20:33.922
And we see how the person reacts.

00:20:33.922 --> 00:20:35.183
Do they get upset?

00:20:35.183 --> 00:20:38.633
Or others are thinking,
why is this guy telling me this?

00:20:38.633 --> 00:20:40.314
What do I need to do differently?

00:20:40.314 --> 00:20:43.180
You can tell that this person's
wheels are turning in their head and

00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:44.844
they're thinking about the problem.

00:20:44.844 --> 00:20:48.040
They're thinking, so that ability
to listen because the market is

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:51.694
always telling you something,
the competitors are telling you something.

00:20:51.694 --> 00:20:55.027
&gt;&gt; So
you want them to have the passion and

00:20:55.027 --> 00:21:00.872
the persuasive power of an evangelist,
but one who also listens?

00:21:00.872 --> 00:21:05.263
&gt;&gt; Yes, because if you don't listen,
then you're stubborn.

00:21:05.263 --> 00:21:08.511
And the risk of being stubborn is,
you can hit your head on the wall and

00:21:08.511 --> 00:21:10.480
never be able to get it across it.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:13.780
&gt;&gt; So another way I would think about this
is, you want someone who's in love with

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:16.932
the problem they're trying to solve,
not with their specific solution.

00:21:16.932 --> 00:21:18.137
&gt;&gt; That is a good way to put it.

00:21:18.137 --> 00:21:22.491
[MUSIC]

00:21:22.491 --> 00:21:25.302
&gt;&gt; Okay, now I'm going to
put you in the other chair.

00:21:25.302 --> 00:21:28.733
I want you to go back to your
days of founding businesses and

00:21:28.733 --> 00:21:30.070
working in startups.

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:35.046
You're in a conversation with
a potential first round HOR seat,

00:21:35.046 --> 00:21:38.561
let's not say seat,
let's say seed or series A investor.

00:21:38.561 --> 00:21:45.830
How should you be assessing this
investor as a potential partner?

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:50.921
&gt;&gt; Again, the starting point is this
investor passionate about this problem?

00:21:50.921 --> 00:21:54.652
Maybe not as much as I am, but
at least has enough passion and

00:21:54.652 --> 00:21:58.609
is thinking about this,
not just from a standpoint of, okay,

00:21:58.609 --> 00:22:01.835
I'll bet behind Sandeep and
he'll make me money.

00:22:01.835 --> 00:22:07.678
So this is a person who has thought about
this problem, has a point of view on it,

00:22:07.678 --> 00:22:11.793
and is therefore able to ask
me intelligent questions.

00:22:11.793 --> 00:22:15.728
&gt;&gt; So, they shouldn't be looking for
someone who just says,

00:22:15.728 --> 00:22:18.493
you convinced me here, but let's do it.

00:22:18.493 --> 00:22:22.779
You actually ought to go past that because
I think a lot of entrepreneurs would be so

00:22:22.779 --> 00:22:23.347
excited.

00:22:23.347 --> 00:22:27.422
If that was the initial response, they
would feel like they just hit a home run.

00:22:27.422 --> 00:22:32.696
&gt;&gt; I would at least ask the person
what about what I said convinced you?

00:22:32.696 --> 00:22:36.820
In a nice way, is there anything
that I should be doing differently?

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:40.524
Is there anything that, what is it
that you think I should do more of?

00:22:40.524 --> 00:22:44.402
At least check that this person
is not a so lazy check writer,

00:22:44.402 --> 00:22:48.674
but is actually going to be a partner
with you through the journey.

00:22:48.674 --> 00:22:50.774
&gt;&gt; Because you want more than the check.

00:22:50.774 --> 00:22:53.597
&gt;&gt; Yes, because see,
any startup is not a straight line.

00:22:53.597 --> 00:22:58.481
You are going to run into problems that
you hadn't got dissipated at the time of

00:22:58.481 --> 00:22:59.523
the fund raise.

00:22:59.523 --> 00:23:03.555
If there is an understanding as
to why that investment was made,

00:23:03.555 --> 00:23:08.124
you can always go back to that and say,
this is what we were looking to do.

00:23:08.124 --> 00:23:10.950
What was wrong with our assumption.

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:15.811
And that ability to do that requires
that there has to be some common set of

00:23:15.811 --> 00:23:19.730
assumptions between the entrepreneur and
the investor.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:24.304
So if you haven't had that
conversation at the start and

00:23:24.304 --> 00:23:29.074
let's say Gerry wrote you a check, then
how do you have this conversation later?

00:23:29.074 --> 00:23:33.624
&gt;&gt; I want to just ask you a little
bit about the due diligence process.

00:23:33.624 --> 00:23:39.391
We talked about valuations don't really
mean much in these early stages,

00:23:39.391 --> 00:23:43.338
but is it a point of
contention in the negotiation?

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:46.989
&gt;&gt; In today's world, yes,
valuations have crept up.

00:23:46.989 --> 00:23:51.965
There is an expectation from
all founders that they should

00:23:51.965 --> 00:23:55.324
get Silicon Valley based valuations.

00:23:55.324 --> 00:24:01.148
Not all founders are targeting very
large markets, equally large markets or

00:24:01.148 --> 00:24:06.093
have the same Capabilities that some of
the returning founders in the Valley or

00:24:06.093 --> 00:24:07.749
even in India might bring.

00:24:07.749 --> 00:24:12.028
So what ends up happening
is they read stories in

00:24:12.028 --> 00:24:16.629
the press that somebody raise $15 million,
and

00:24:16.629 --> 00:24:20.485
sees a round at a 35 pre money valuation.

00:24:20.485 --> 00:24:25.524
And they think that, hey, if they can
do that, maybe I'm not as good, but

00:24:25.524 --> 00:24:31.149
maybe I'll get a 25 or 35, and the real
clearing price is 10, right, or 8.

00:24:31.149 --> 00:24:35.948
There is some level of expectation
management that needs to

00:24:35.948 --> 00:24:39.214
happen in even early stage valuations.

00:24:39.214 --> 00:24:41.914
&gt;&gt; You've talked with so
many founders and evaluated so

00:24:41.914 --> 00:24:44.083
many companies that are raising capital.

00:24:44.083 --> 00:24:47.769
What are the most common mistakes
that entrepreneurs make during

00:24:47.769 --> 00:24:51.805
those early fundraising stages,
pre-seed and early stage funding?

00:24:51.805 --> 00:24:55.694
&gt;&gt; So one of the things I
tell all my founders is

00:24:55.694 --> 00:25:00.571
don't go to market stating
valuation expectation.

00:25:00.571 --> 00:25:05.586
You can share with the market what your
last round post money was, you can share

00:25:05.586 --> 00:25:10.983
with the market how your company has grown
from the last round, but leave it at that.

00:25:10.983 --> 00:25:15.944
Have the market tell you what
they think is a fair valuation.

00:25:15.944 --> 00:25:21.197
Second is, it is important for
you to have clarity on what

00:25:21.197 --> 00:25:27.880
are the risks that you're going to
face going forward in your business.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:32.354
We see a lot of situations where
entrepreneurs come expecting

00:25:32.354 --> 00:25:37.095
everything to be hunky dory all the time,
and it isn't the case.

00:25:37.095 --> 00:25:40.517
It means that they're not good listeners,
they're not so seeing,

00:25:40.517 --> 00:25:43.417
okay, here's a potential
problem that I could run into.

00:25:43.417 --> 00:25:47.968
You should be able to imagine that
once you had the money in your bank,

00:25:47.968 --> 00:25:49.703
what would you do with it?

00:25:49.703 --> 00:25:53.629
And what are the risks that you would see,
and how you would mitigate those risks.

00:25:53.629 --> 00:25:55.830
We have people that come to us for
funding, and

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:59.167
they haven't even understood what
their competitive landscape is.

00:25:59.167 --> 00:26:02.553
And that, I think, is just sloppiness.

00:26:02.553 --> 00:26:07.383
So those are the kinds of issues
that a founder should have

00:26:07.383 --> 00:26:10.716
a few conversations with friendlies.

00:26:10.716 --> 00:26:16.109
Obviously, talking to your own existing
investors and using them as a devil's

00:26:16.109 --> 00:26:20.792
advocate is very valuable, and
existing investors should do that.

00:26:20.792 --> 00:26:25.812
They should not try and
be cheerleaders for the founders.

00:26:25.812 --> 00:26:30.897
But in these situations,
they should be very objective and

00:26:30.897 --> 00:26:34.058
play the role of external investor.

00:26:34.058 --> 00:26:38.446
It's a tricky thing to do because you
don't want to have that person get

00:26:38.446 --> 00:26:43.421
upset at you for you saying something,
but it's important that that person hear

00:26:43.421 --> 00:26:46.954
it from you before they hear
it from excellent investor.

00:26:46.954 --> 00:26:50.792
[MUSIC]

00:26:50.792 --> 00:26:54.185
&gt;&gt; I'm just curious, is there
a question that you wish I'd asked you?

00:26:54.185 --> 00:26:56.048
What am I missing here?

00:26:56.048 --> 00:27:00.010
&gt;&gt; How do you look at various
opportunities that come to you, right?

00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:03.091
So today,
I can have an opportunity that's coming in

00:27:03.091 --> 00:27:06.038
a company that's building
the next marketplace,

00:27:06.038 --> 00:27:10.917
it could be an opportunity in a company
that's building the next education portal.

00:27:10.917 --> 00:27:16.321
There's a company that's coming to me,
that has the next content play.

00:27:16.321 --> 00:27:21.794
Now as a investor, I have to make choices,
because as a fund,

00:27:21.794 --> 00:27:29.402
we are looking to invest across 25 to 27
companies in a fund to create a portfolio.

00:27:29.402 --> 00:27:33.854
So I'm looking at either the stage
of the company, I'm looking at

00:27:33.854 --> 00:27:39.119
the industry in which they're playing,
so I'm making a overall portfolio.

00:27:39.119 --> 00:27:42.969
And so sometimes, I may have too much
of a weightage in a particular area,

00:27:42.969 --> 00:27:45.513
in which case,
I may not be able to invest in you,

00:27:45.513 --> 00:27:48.394
nothing wrong with the company
that you're running.

00:27:48.394 --> 00:27:53.173
Sometimes, you see three great companies
come to you around the same time.

00:27:53.173 --> 00:27:54.740
How do you make a choice?

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:58.581
You're basically saying I can only back
one, I only have capacity to do one.

00:27:58.581 --> 00:28:00.839
How do I make that choice?

00:28:00.839 --> 00:28:05.760
And that's where it's very
important from a founder

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:10.897
perspective to get to the right VC and
have a point of view

00:28:10.897 --> 00:28:16.163
as to this fund is more likely
to invest in a contemplate.

00:28:16.163 --> 00:28:21.603
This fund or this partner is
more likely to back marketplace.

00:28:21.603 --> 00:28:29.507
And try and get as much information about
the individual, not just about the firm.

00:28:29.507 --> 00:28:32.426
It's very important that you think
through and look at the partners,

00:28:32.426 --> 00:28:35.408
look at what the partners are doing,
look at what their interests are.

00:28:35.408 --> 00:28:40.840
What have they spoken about in the last
six months about their areas of interest?

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:44.405
People talk about, hey,
I'm looking at investing in this area,

00:28:44.405 --> 00:28:46.990
you need to sort of stay on top of that.

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:53.215
As a founder, I think going in
prepared with an understanding

00:28:53.215 --> 00:28:58.363
of how the investor ecosystem
looks like can be very

00:28:58.363 --> 00:29:03.170
helpful in an efficient
fundraising process.

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:06.556
&gt;&gt; So
how many deals do you look at a year?

00:29:06.556 --> 00:29:09.805
And of those,
how many do you actually invest in?

00:29:09.805 --> 00:29:13.011
And then I want to talk a little
bit about the ones who turned away.

00:29:13.011 --> 00:29:17.717
&gt;&gt; Normally,
we would do 7 to 8 deals a year.

00:29:17.717 --> 00:29:20.353
These are series A investments.

00:29:20.353 --> 00:29:25.721
So we will probably do an equal amount,
maybe more as seeds.

00:29:25.721 --> 00:29:31.852
So in the course of a year, we'll probably
end up doing about 20 investments or

00:29:31.852 --> 00:29:35.729
18 investments out of
which 8 would be series A,

00:29:35.729 --> 00:29:38.817
an 8 to 10 would be seed investments.

00:29:38.817 --> 00:29:43.951
Our series A investments would
range between, say 3 to 7 million,

00:29:43.951 --> 00:29:50.338
and our seed investments would typically
be anywhere between 250k to 1 million.

00:29:50.338 --> 00:29:53.094
Sometimes we'll go slightly higher.

00:29:53.094 --> 00:29:57.686
We expect that in our seed investments,
maybe a fifth of them or

00:29:57.686 --> 00:30:02.381
a quarter of them would make it
to a series A, and others won't.

00:30:02.381 --> 00:30:06.217
So these are very fundamental
risks that we're taking.

00:30:06.217 --> 00:30:11.055
And to get to this number,
we will probably see

00:30:11.055 --> 00:30:15.903
on average 500 to 600 companies a year.

00:30:15.903 --> 00:30:17.898
&gt;&gt; So you're saying no an awful lot?

00:30:17.898 --> 00:30:21.827
&gt;&gt; Yes, and
what we do is we say no quickly, and

00:30:21.827 --> 00:30:24.815
we know we will get things wrong.

00:30:24.815 --> 00:30:28.235
For us, a lot has to do with
the founder of the team.

00:30:28.235 --> 00:30:33.987
So, sometimes, you can look at a plan and
say, the space doesn't make any sense.

00:30:33.987 --> 00:30:38.948
But something about that plan says I
should at least talk to the person.

00:30:38.948 --> 00:30:43.271
So we will do a call with the person,
and sometimes you say,

00:30:43.271 --> 00:30:47.765
this may not be the right problem
that he or she is solving, but

00:30:47.765 --> 00:30:50.894
they are just a great, just amazing team.

00:30:50.894 --> 00:30:54.447
So let's go back and look at a seed
investment and see where they go.

00:30:54.447 --> 00:30:58.760
[MUSIC]

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:01.731
&gt;&gt; What I find fascinating
about this is the seed round,

00:31:01.731 --> 00:31:05.176
the personality of the founder and
the team are so important.

00:31:05.176 --> 00:31:08.906
When you're letting them down,
when you're in the room with them and

00:31:08.906 --> 00:31:12.454
you're going to say no, in a sense,
it must be hard for them to take.

00:31:12.454 --> 00:31:16.937
Because you're saying no to them based
on their personality and who they are.

00:31:16.937 --> 00:31:19.582
And do they understand that and
how do they respond?

00:31:19.582 --> 00:31:23.765
I mean, I would love to hear
an example of an unsuccessful pitch and

00:31:23.765 --> 00:31:27.215
how you handle it, and
how the founder accepted that.

00:31:27.215 --> 00:31:32.302
&gt;&gt; Normally, we will let the founder know
that either it is because of the space or

00:31:32.302 --> 00:31:36.771
it is because we felt that there was
something in the way that they were

00:31:36.771 --> 00:31:40.780
presenting or the way they were
taking the solution forward,

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:43.199
that we weren't comfortable with.

00:31:43.199 --> 00:31:45.441
&gt;&gt; It's not your role to
be their psychiatrist.

00:31:45.441 --> 00:31:47.581
&gt;&gt; No, but we will say, hey, listen,

00:31:47.581 --> 00:31:51.381
we think that you are too focused
on marketing and this needs you to

00:31:51.381 --> 00:31:55.549
build a better product first before
we would spend money on marketing.

00:31:55.549 --> 00:31:58.447
Or it could be that you need
to build a team around you.

00:31:58.447 --> 00:32:02.840
It's an interesting idea, but
we just don't see a team.

00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:04.450
Can execute an instinct.

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:07.603
Sometimes that person will say,
thank you for the feedback, goes and

00:32:07.603 --> 00:32:10.720
works on it for the next three months and
then comes back.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:16.101
Others will just say, don't want
to deal with this, let's move on.

00:32:16.101 --> 00:32:20.154
&gt;&gt; So, can you share a story of
a time when you didn't invest and

00:32:20.154 --> 00:32:21.913
then regretted it later.

00:32:21.913 --> 00:32:26.880
&gt;&gt; So, we did not put money into Ola.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.230
Ola is India's equivalent of Uber, right.

00:32:29.230 --> 00:32:32.820
So, Ola is the largest
player in the taxi space.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:38.294
And the reason why we didn't do it is
because when he initially started,

00:32:38.294 --> 00:32:43.513
the regulatory framework did not
allow Taxi hailing through an app.

00:32:43.513 --> 00:32:47.726
And so, there was a risk of
either director liability or

00:32:47.726 --> 00:32:53.210
potentially this thing being
shut down by the government.

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:54.668
And so, we didn't want to take that risk.

00:32:54.668 --> 00:33:01.180
We did not invest in fresh works
at this point other than Zoho.

00:33:01.180 --> 00:33:03.216
It's India's largest sort of software.

00:33:03.216 --> 00:33:07.591
SAS company,
because our view was that they were at

00:33:07.591 --> 00:33:11.370
the point that they came to for
investment.

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:13.820
They were effectively a copy of Zendesk.

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:18.044
Interestingly, the initial
growth that happened was because

00:33:18.044 --> 00:33:21.060
the Zendesk founder wrote a rad.

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:25.061
And complained about how Fresh desk
just totally copied the Desk and

00:33:25.061 --> 00:33:27.809
how they were just trying
to compete on price.

00:33:27.809 --> 00:33:32.295
And any customer that was looking for
a helpdesk solution at the time and

00:33:32.295 --> 00:33:35.608
felt that Desk was too
expensive went to Fresh desk.

00:33:35.608 --> 00:33:39.331
[LAUGH] So, and for
me is as important as right?

00:33:39.331 --> 00:33:42.900
You take what you get even if
they're copying something.

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:44.250
That is global.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:46.312
How will the differentiated and

00:33:46.312 --> 00:33:51.330
garish had a clear path of how they
would differentiate themselves?

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:53.060
Visa we Zendesk and they've done that.

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:57.140
&gt;&gt; And that brings us to
the end of today's masterclass.

00:33:57.140 --> 00:34:01.020
I want to thank Sandeep Singh for
sharing his insights with us today.

00:34:01.020 --> 00:34:05.327
From Sandeep's perspective, early stage
investment decisions are often based less

00:34:05.327 --> 00:34:08.550
on the numbers and
more on the people involved.

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:12.522
For entrepreneurs to attract strong
partners, they need to demonstrate real

00:34:12.522 --> 00:34:15.530
insight into the competitive
landscape of their market and

00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:18.530
to build businesses that
solve problems at scale.

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:20.349
Founders need to have
a strong internal drive.

00:34:21.410 --> 00:34:25.010
For Sandeep, what he looks for
an entrepreneur is clear.

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:26.364
&gt;&gt; Number one, his energy level.

00:34:26.364 --> 00:34:30.771
That individual when they're presenting
to me should get me as excited about

00:34:30.771 --> 00:34:32.207
the problem as they are.

